From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 02:32:18 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] nvme-apple: Wait for a completion that lands after its
 interrupt

On the M4 Pro (T6040) a write sometimes stalled for the full 30 s I/O
timeout and then completed with "I/O 2(aq:0) timeout in state 1:
completion polled". The completion entry was in the queue by the time
the timeout handler looked, so the command had finished long before.
In the field this delayed suspend entry by 19 s and stalled writers
such as speakerguardd. A 4 KiB random write load on btrfs with the
"none" scheduler reproduces it about once every 100 s.

Instrumenting the interrupt handler showed that the stalls follow
interrupts that find both completion queues empty. ANS raises one
interrupt per posted entry and can raise it before the entry is visible
to the CPU. No second interrupt follows, so the handler returns
IRQ_NONE and the command waits for the timeout. Entries posted while the
handler is running are not affected: each one got its own interrupt
within 14 us (1881 of 1881 in one 10 minute run).

When an interrupt finds both queues empty, poll them for up to 100 us
before treating it as spurious. In the instrumented runs the late entry
appeared 2-16 us after the interrupt. The common path, which finds an
entry straight away, is unchanged. The wait is skipped while both
queues are disabled.

Measured on the J616s (M4 Pro), 10 minutes of the reproducer each,
same kernel source apart from the handler:
- without the wait: 6 stalls of 30 s, 8 empty interrupts in 5.3M
- with the wait: 0 stalls, 9 late entries found after 2-16 us, 2 empty
  interrupts in 9.4M with no entry (spurious)

The M1 Pro, M2 and A18 Pro run this handler too and have not shown the
stall in their last 60 boots. The change costs them nothing unless an
interrupt arrives with nothing to complete.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(cherry picked from commit 7f12ac1acddfdfd393487bc9a6afaec7cc9be30e)
---
 drivers/nvme/host/apple.c | 50 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
index 77367f8d8d..d4927635fa 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/blk-crypto-profile.h>
 #include <linux/blkdev.h>
 #include <linux/blk-mq.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/dmapool.h>
@@ -24,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/jiffies.h>
 #include <linux/kref.h>
+#include <linux/ktime.h>
 #include <linux/mempool.h>
 #include <linux/mm.h>
 #include <linux/overflow.h>
@@ -985,22 +987,54 @@ static bool apple_nvme_handle_cq(struct apple_nvme_queue *q, bool force)
 	return found;
 }
 
+static bool apple_nvme_handle_queues(struct apple_nvme *anv)
+{
+	bool found = apple_nvme_handle_cq(&anv->ioq, false);
+
+	found |= apple_nvme_handle_cq(&anv->adminq, false);
+	return found;
+}
+
+/*
+ * ANS raises one completion interrupt per posted entry, and the interrupt can
+ * reach the CPU before the entry is visible in the completion queue. On T6040
+ * the entry showed up 2-16 us after an interrupt that found both queues
+ * empty. No second interrupt follows, so returning at that point leaves the
+ * command waiting for the I/O timeout. Give a late entry time to land.
+ */
+#define APPLE_NVME_LATE_CQE_US	100
+
+static bool apple_nvme_wait_late_cqe(struct apple_nvme *anv)
+{
+	ktime_t end;
+
+	if (!apple_nvme_queue_enabled(&anv->ioq) &&
+	    !apple_nvme_queue_enabled(&anv->adminq))
+		return false;
+
+	end = ktime_add_us(ktime_get(), APPLE_NVME_LATE_CQE_US);
+	do {
+		udelay(1);
+		if (apple_nvme_handle_queues(anv))
+			return true;
+	} while (ktime_before(ktime_get(), end));
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 static irqreturn_t apple_nvme_irq(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct apple_nvme *anv = data;
-	bool handled = false;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	bool handled;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&anv->lock, flags);
-	if (apple_nvme_handle_cq(&anv->ioq, false))
-		handled = true;
-	if (apple_nvme_handle_cq(&anv->adminq, false))
-		handled = true;
+	handled = apple_nvme_handle_queues(anv);
+	if (!handled)
+		handled = apple_nvme_wait_late_cqe(anv);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&anv->lock, flags);
 
-	if (handled)
-		return IRQ_HANDLED;
-	return IRQ_NONE;
+	return handled ? IRQ_HANDLED : IRQ_NONE;
 }
 
 static int apple_nvme_create_cq(struct apple_nvme *anv)
